---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Hair Maintenance Guide: Everyday Habits Between Cuts"
  description="Simple everyday habits that keep hair healthy and shape intact between salon visits."
  canonicalPath="/hair-care/hair-maintenance/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Care" title="Hair Maintenance Between Cuts" image="/images/gallery/gallery-private-studio-1.jpg" imageAlt="Minimalist private studio interior" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / <a href="/hair-care/" class="hover:underline">Hair Care</a> / Hair Maintenance</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      What you do between salon visits matters just as much as the visits
      themselves. A great haircut can look tired within weeks if daily habits work
      against it, while a handful of small, consistent choices can keep hair
      looking freshly cut and healthy well past your next scheduled trim.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Protect Hair While You Sleep</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Sleep on a silk or satin pillowcase to reduce friction and breakage — cotton
      pillowcases create more drag against hair overnight, which can roughen the
      cuticle and contribute to frizz and tangling over time. If a full pillowcase
      swap isn't in the budget, a silk or satin bonnet or scarf offers similar
      protection.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Be Mindful of Tension</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Avoid tight hairstyles worn daily, which can stress the hairline over time —
      consistently tight ponytails, buns, or braids pull on the same points of the
      scalp repeatedly, and that sustained tension is a well-documented contributor
      to hairline thinning in some people. Rotating between tighter and looser
      styles, and giving hair regular breaks from tension, helps reduce that risk.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Stay Ahead of Trims</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Book trims on a regular cadence rather than waiting until ends look visibly
      damaged — split ends don't repair themselves, and once a split travels far
      enough up the hair shaft, more length has to be cut to fully remove it.
      Getting ahead of that with a proactive schedule, even if it's just a small
      trim, usually means less length lost overall in the long run.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Brush and Detangle Gently</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Brushing from the ends upward, rather than dragging a brush from root to tip
      through a full tangle, reduces breakage significantly. A wide-tooth comb or a
      detangling brush designed for wet hair is gentler than a standard brush,
      especially on textured or curly hair, which is more prone to breakage when
      combed dry.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Match Products to Your Actual Hair, Not Just Trends</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      It's easy to accumulate a shelf of products based on what's trending rather
      than what your specific hair type needs. Fine hair generally does better with
      lightweight, low-buildup formulas; thick or coarse hair usually tolerates —
      and needs — richer products. If you're not sure what category your hair falls
      into, ask your stylist directly next time you're in for a cut.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Plan Ahead for Salon Visits</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Use a <a href="/tools/hair-length-calculator/" class="text-rose-600 underline">hair length calculator</a>
      or a simple calendar reminder to stay on top of your ideal trim schedule
      rather than booking reactively. Consistent timing, more than any single
      product, is usually the biggest factor separating hair that maintains its
      shape well and hair that doesn't.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Small Habits That Add Up</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      None of these habits work miracles on their own, but stacked together over
      months they tend to be the real difference between hair that consistently
      looks healthy between salon visits and hair that looks tired by week three.
      Pick one or two changes to start with rather than overhauling your entire
      routine at once — consistency with a couple of habits beats a perfect
      routine you abandon after a week.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Diet, Sleep, and Overall Health</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Hair is often one of the last places the body invests resources, which is
      why overall health — adequate protein, sleep, and hydration — shows up
      indirectly in hair quality over time. This isn't a substitute for a proper
      hair-care routine, but it's worth mentioning: no product routine fully
      compensates for months of poor sleep or a very restrictive diet, and
      unexplained changes in hair texture or shedding are sometimes worth
      mentioning to a doctor rather than treating as a purely cosmetic problem.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      See also: <a href="/tools/hair-length-calculator/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Hair Length Calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Small, Steady Effort</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      None of these habits require much extra time — a silk pillowcase, gentler
      brushing, and a regular trim schedule are all low-effort changes that
      compound into a noticeably healthier result over several months.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
